I see a potential issue in `fu_coswid_firmware_parse_entity`:

```c
} else if (tag_id == FU_COSWID_TAG_ROLE) {
        if (cbor_isa_uint(pairs[i].value)) {
                FuCoswidEntityRole role = cbor_get_uint8(pairs[i].value);
                entity->roles[entity_role_cnt++] = role;
        }
        for (guint j = 0; j < cbor_array_size(pairs[i].value); j++) {
                ...
        }
}
```

If `pairs[i].value` is a uint, `cbor_array_size(pairs[i].value)` is called on a non-array item. This could cause issues. But more importantly, if `pairs[i].value` is a uint, `entity->roles[entity_role_cnt++] = role;` is executed without checking if `entity_role_cnt` is within bounds. The `roles` array has 6 elements (`FuCoswidEntityRole roles[6]`). If there are more than 6 uint roles, this would be a stack-buffer-overflow, not heap.

But wait, the issue says "Heap-buffer-overflow READ 8". Let me think about this differently.

Actually, looking at the `fu_coswid_firmware_parse_hash` function more carefully:

```c
static gboolean
fu_coswid_firmware_parse_hash(FuCoswidFirmware *self,
                              cbor_item_t *item,
                              gpointer user_data,
                              GError **error)
{
        FuCoswidFirmwarePayload *payload = (FuCoswidFirmwarePayload *)user_data;
        g_autoptr(FuCoswidFirmwareHash) hash = g_new0(FuCoswidFirmwareHash, 1);
        g_autoptr(cbor_item_t) hash_item_alg_id = cbor_array_get(item, 0);
        g_autoptr(cbor_item_t) hash_item_value = cbor_array_get(item, 1);
```

The `cbor_array_get(item, 0)` and `cbor_array_get(item, 1)` are called on `item`. If `item` is not an array, this could be a problem. But in the call chain from `fu_coswid_firmware_parse_file`, `item` is either:
1. `pairs[i].value` which is checked to be an array (in the else branch)
2. An element from `fu_coswid_firmware_parse_hash_array`, which iterates over elements of an array - these elements could be anything

So the vulnerability is in case 2: if an element of the hash array is not itself an array, then `cbor_array_get(item, 0)` on a non-array item could cause a heap-buffer-overflow.

